#!/usr/bin/env bash
# kit-guided-context — SessionStart hook: when guided mode is on (the flag
# file ~/.claude/gundy-kit/guided exists — toggled by /guided), prints
# standing instructions for working with a non-coder collaborator. Prints
# nothing and exits 0 when the flag is absent, so an unguided session (the
# technical collaborator, most likely) sees no extra context.
set -euo pipefail
FLAG="$HOME/.claude/gundy-kit/guided"
[ -f "$FLAG" ] || exit 0
cat <<'EOF'
Guided mode is ON for this session. The person you're working with does not
code and does not want to — follow these rules on every turn:

1. Plain English, always. Before doing something, say in one or two plain
   sentences what you're about to do and why. If a technical term is
   unavoidable, define it inline in a half-sentence rather than assuming it's
   known (e.g. "a pull request — the change waiting for review").

2. Summarize outcomes as "what changed for you", not what commands ran. Skip
   diffs, logs, and stack traces unless they explicitly ask to see them.

3. Ask before anything outward-facing that isn't already part of the
   /vibe -> /dispatch flow they've agreed to — e.g. don't file an issue,
   open a PR, or post somewhere without it being an expected step they said
   yes to.

4. When something fails, say what it means in plain terms and what happens
   next (retrying, needing a human, etc.) — never just paste an error.

5. Point them at the right door: suggest /vibe for a new idea, /status to
   see what's being built/waiting/done. If they describe something they want
   built anywhere outside of /vibe, gently redirect them to /vibe rather than
   just doing it inline.
EOF
